it's a mammoth task  mapping photographing and referencing  millions of gravestones in thousands of  graveyards the goal to provide a digital  map of burial sites and records that  will be invaluable to historians and  genealogists and fascinating to the  public  we're here in barton churchyard near  penrith in cumbria and there we can see  the headstones we can see the trees we  can see the graves and again imagining  we're overseas somewhere there we have a  photograph  of that very headstone that we can see  here  obviously these are now protected  because there's  numerous stories of records being lost  through fire or theft or floods up here  in particular for example so they could  be lost with flooding we also have a  copy of the original burial register so  they're protected and also having them  online means people are not having to  manhandle them so they're not being  they're not deteriorating through  through heavy use and abuse and that  sort of thing and as you say they're  accessible from anywhere in the world  exactly  the backpacks the team used to collect  the data cost a hundred thousand pounds  each and contain five cameras two laser  scanners and the latest gps technology  it may only take an hour or so to scan a  graveyard like this but with 50 million  measurements being gathered it can take  up to a week to process that information  and with 19 000 graveyards to be mapped  the seven year timescale for this  project could be a tight one  once the initial data has been fed into  the system the local parishes themselves  can keep the online records updated the  survey put into context everything  within the churchyard the building the  headstones and even the vegetation  and what we learned at that point was  where the old maps weren't exact in in  scale in detail so we've had that  immediate advantage  there are benefits for ecologists  biologists and botanists too the data  gathered by this study will also be used  as part of a national biodiversity  survey of the sometimes very rare plants  and animals that are living amongst our  dead  fraser moored sky news penrith
 

